145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (This project showed me that architecture matters more than features. When we designed the base classes the rest of the game grew naturally without having to rewrite anything from scratch.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (Working with SCRUM taught me to commit to a realistic scope. Several times I wanted to add new mechanics to the game, but the sprint had clear objectives and that forced me to prioritize what actually mattered.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (The merge conflict problem was frustrating at first, but it made me actually learn Git: feature branches, PRs, rebase. I can no longer imagine working on a team project any other way.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (My conclusion is that a video game is a system of systems. Each module needs to work independently and communicate with the rest through well-defined interfaces. That applies equally to any software project.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
